WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.960
So, it's 5 o'clock in New York City and I’m
about to catch a cab about

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.820
5 miles uptown.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.040
Let's see how this goes.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:31.440
So it just took me 40 minutes to go about 4.5 miles which is pretty typical for New York City rush hour.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.199
Despite a speed limit of 25 miles per hour,
the average car moving through NYC is driving

00:00:37.199 --> 00:00:42.710
at just 7.1 MPH, down from 9.1 MPH in 2010.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.199
And if you’re in midtown it’s even
worse, with cars moving around 5 MPH.

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:52.050
But it’s not just New York City – traffic
in cities like LA is so bad drivers could

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.359
be locked in gridlock for hours.

00:00:54.359 --> 00:00:59.729
Of course this sucks for drivers, but it also makes
activities like biking or walking less safe

00:00:59.729 --> 00:01:04.240
because cyclists and pedestrians have to weave
through an obstacle course of cars.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:10.320
Not to mention the estimated 20 billion dollars
in lost revenue due to wasted time sitting in traffic.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:17.380
Now, there might be a solution, but if you
commute by car, you are probably not gonna like it.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.880
It’s called congestion pricing.
And it means charging drivers for using the roads.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:31.240
"Congestion pricing is an idea whose time has come. And I believe this is the year to actually get it done."

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.190
New York’s plan is still in the works, and
it probably won’t be enacted until 2020.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:40.450
But the end game is to reduce congestion by
discouraging people from driving if they have

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:44.600
other options like biking, or taking a train, or walking.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.240
And to fund public transit at the same time.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.670
It’s not a groundbreaking idea: congestion
pricing is already old news in cities around

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:53.440
the world.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.820
London enacted a similar policy in 2003.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:00.970
This is a necessary step for us to reclaim
some of the space that is currently given

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:04.350
to a motorized vehicles without ending up
with gridlock.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.220
Nicole Badstuber researches urban infrastructure

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:12.040
and policy at the University of Cambridge
and according to her, the system's pretty simple.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:17.640
When drivers enter the Central London congestion
zone between 7 a.m. and 6 p.m., they’re

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.180
charged 11 pounds 50 pence – about 15 US dollars.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.800
New York City's plan will be similar.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.670
When drivers enter Midtown or Lower Manhattan,
they'll face a fee.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:31.670
There's cameras all around the roads at the
edges of the congestion charging area.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.540
They automatically recognize the name plate
of the car or the vehicle entering the zone.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.220
London has a few exemptions in place, like

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.770
for people who live inside the congestion
zone or vehicles with 9 or more seats and

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:47.840
New York City will likely do that, too.
And the system works.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.020
So since it was introduced, we've seen that private
vehicles

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.120
entering the zone have gone down by 40 percent.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.260
Overall vehicle traffic has gone down by 25
percent.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:02.660
Cycling overall has increased 66% since the
charge was instituted and bus ridership reached

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.800
a 50-year high in 2011.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.180
And wait times for buses decreased 25%,
due to increased service both on buses and

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.630
on the London Underground.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:15.100
So we now, in comparison, still have much higher frequencies of London Underground services.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:20.819
We can get more people, more capacity, more people into our trains because we have newer trains.

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:25.670
And like Nicole said, congestion pricing isn’t
just about removing cars from specific zones,

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.380
it’s about reclaiming a space for the public.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:34.120
Picture Trafalgar Square, but designed for cars
– an idea that was very much a reality before

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.620
congestion pricing.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:40.760
You would basically have a bus driving right
past your nose as you come out of the National Gallery

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.690
Reclaiming that section of road made the square
safer and opened it to more public events.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:49.280
No one could imagine going back to what it
was before, and having these cars and buses

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:50.220
zoom past you.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.040
London’s plan is widely embraced today, but
it was met with resistance at first, with

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:01.410
opponents arguing that congestion pricing
could cut people off from health care, shopping,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:02.410
and schools.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:06.310
Plus, people had to trust that the government
would work efficiently and make significant

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.260
improvements to their public transit system.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:13.740
But within a year, London's congestion charging
had majority support.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.310
As New York’s plan is being finalized, some
similar resistance is cropping up, which

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.400
isn’t too surprising.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.910
After all, it’s the first US city to implement this
type of congestion pricing and no one

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:27.560
wants to pay for something they’ve gotten
for free for so long.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.060
But the plan could generate up to a billion
dollars for public transit, a system that

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.340
most agree desperately needs repair.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:38.020
And the city estimates it will reduce congestion
by 8 to 13 percent and increase speeds by

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.240
up to 9 percent, making a ride through midtown a
lot easier.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:48.260
So, like other cities where congestion pricing
has been successful, it’s likely that people

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.280
will end up accepting it.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:55.090
When we think of our roads, in particular in
cities, as a sort of public good, as a public

00:04:55.090 --> 00:05:01.620
space, then if you're taking up more of it
you should probably be paying for that privilege.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:06.080
If you start to think about how everyone gets
around the city, charging cars begins to make a lot

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.120
more sense: You pay for parking, pay for the
subway, pay to take a train or a bus, so

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.240
why wouldn’t we pay for 
a city road?

